---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="AI-friendly context compression - Marionette" lang="en" languageSwitcherPath="/marionette/ja/ai-friendly/">
  <h1>AI-friendly context compression</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Marionette is AI-friendly because it reduces cross-stack context and gives AI tools fewer boundaries to reason across.
  </p>

  <section class="top-section" aria-labelledby="context-compression-en">
    <h2 id="context-compression-en">Context compression, not unverified token claims</h2>
    <p>
      Marionette does not lead with unverified token metrics. Instead, it focuses on context compression: screens, state transitions, and action handlers stay close together in Go, while htmx handles browser-side partial updates.
    </p>
    <p>
      This reduces cross-stack context, creates fewer boundaries between backend and frontend work, and leaves less schema handoff for both humans and AI tools to reconstruct.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="top-section" aria-labelledby="ai-workflow-en">
    <h2 id="ai-workflow-en">What changes in an AI coding workflow</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Less context switching:</strong> route logic, state updates, and UI composition are described in one Go-first flow.</li>
      <li><strong>Fewer boundaries:</strong> teams avoid repeatedly explaining separate client state, API contracts, and backend handlers for the same admin screen.</li>
      <li><strong>Less schema handoff:</strong> component and action definitions stay close to the server-side behavior they trigger.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      As a secondary effect, this can make token consumption easier to keep under control because prompts can focus on product behavior instead of re-explaining cross-stack coordination.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
